{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment.** The test adequately checks the subject. The subject's one distinctive obligation is that, for uncovered end-user-observable behavior, the skill directs agents to draft a requirement and then take it through review, agreement and commit before implementing. The reviewed test checks that routing. The pipeline it routes into is checked by sibling tests.\n\n**What the subject requires**\n- When behavior no requirement covers shows up, the `habeas-requirements` skill sends the agent to drafting.\n- The draft then goes through review, agreement and commit.\n- All of that happens before the behavior is implemented.\n\n**What the marked test checks**\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` makes two assertions:\n- `assert_phrases` requires that the bundled skill text contain \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2\". That sentence is in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section.\n- `step(\"habeas-requirements\", 2).starts_with(\"Draft.\")` confirms that step 2 is the drafting step.\n\nTogether these show the skill directs uncovered behavior to drafting. Because the workflow is sequential, drafting at step 2 flows into step 3 (review), step 4 (present and agree), step 5 (commit) and step 6 (implement).\n\n**How the rest of the pipeline is checked**\nThese checks sit in sibling tests marked for the parent or sibling requirements, not for the subject.\n- `the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change`, marked for `text_change`, asserts that step 3 starts with \"Review.\", step 4 with \"Present\" and step 5 with \"Commit.\".\n- `the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code`, marked for `requirements_review_skill`, asserts the order draft < review < gate < commit < implement.\n- `the_requirements_skill_gates_commit_and_code_on_every_group_being_decided` asserts the Gate text: no implementation, test code or commit until the user has agreed to, rejected or deferred every group.\n\nWith these in place, the subject test does not need to repeat the pipeline. Those tests do not carry the subject's {test} marking, but the subject's own obligation is the routing, and its own test checks that.\n\n**Weakness**\nThe phrase check is a substring match, so it would not catch a rewording that appended something like \"then implement\" to that sentence. That is an unlikely variation and not something the text names, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap.",
